    <p class="flavor">
      There is no initiative roll. There are no turns. When a fight starts, everyone acts
      at once &mdash; choosing, committing, and living with the consequences on a shared
      timeline. Welcome to the timing track.
    </p>

    {% handler %}
      <p>I lost three operatives before I understood how a real fight works. Everything happens at once. The fastest person in the room isn't the one who hits hardest — they're the one who gets to choose while everyone else is still reacting. I've watched a knife fighter with quick hands carve up a gunman who thought his revolver made him king. It didn't. He was still drawing when the blade found his ribs.</p>
    {% endhandler %}

    {% plate "market-fight", "A young caster throws up a glowing cyan shield in a sunlit market square as gunmen fire and fruit flies", "A fight in the market square, frozen on the timing track.", "full" %}

    <h3 id="timing-track">The Timing Track</h3>

    {% spot "timing", "A hunting knife, revolver cartridges, and an open pocket watch on a wooden crate" %}

    <p>
      When a situation turns violent &mdash; or when timing and sequencing matter &mdash;
      the GM calls for the timing track. Think of it as a number line starting at zero.
      Every action takes a certain number of <strong>counts</strong>, and you place a
      token on the count when your action will <strong>complete</strong>.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>The GM sets the scene.</strong> Where are you? What's around you? How far apart is everyone? Weapons drawn or holstered?</li>
      <li><strong>Everyone declares their first action at once.</strong> Nobody has seen anyone else act yet &mdash; you're reacting to the situation, not to each other.</li>
      <li><strong>Place tokens.</strong> Drawing a sword takes 2 counts, so your token goes on count 2. Firing a revolver takes 3 counts &mdash; token on count 3. Casting a spell might take 3 or 4, depending on the spell.</li>
      <li><strong>The GM advances the track.</strong> Count by count. When it reaches your token, your action resolves.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3 id="who-goes-when">Who Goes When?</h3>

    <p>
      When multiple tokens land on the same count, whoever <strong>started their action
      first</strong> resolves first. If two people started on the same count, they resolve
      <strong>truly simultaneously</strong> &mdash; both shots land, both spells fire, neither
      can react to the other.
    </p>

    <p>
      After actions resolve, characters who need to declare new actions do so in order of
      <strong>PC (Physical Coordination), lowest first</strong>. Ties broken by AW, lowest
      first. This means the fastest, most coordinated characters <strong>declare last</strong> &mdash;
      they see what the opposition is committing to before they choose their response.
    </p>

    {% example "Why This Matters" %}
      <p>Kael has PC +2. A thug has PC +0. When both need to declare on the same count, the thug
      commits first &mdash; he's pulling a knife. Kael <em>sees this</em> and then decides: draw
      his knife and meet the charge, or step back and clear his revolver? The thug is locked
      in. Kael gets to react. That's a significant advantage, and it's a real reason to invest in
      PC and AW at character creation.</p>
    {% endexample %}

    <h3 id="changing-your-mind">Changing Your Mind</h3>

    <p>
      You can abandon your current action at any time before it resolves. The counts you've
      already spent are gone, and your new action takes its normal speed <strong>plus a
      1-count penalty</strong>. Sometimes it's worth eating that penalty to respond to a
      changing situation.
    </p>

    <h3 id="making-an-attack">Making an Attack</h3>

    <p>
      Steel meets flesh. A bullet finds its mark. Here's what happens when your action
      token hits the count.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Roll d100 against your skill target</strong> &mdash; Melee, Ranged, or Firearms. Under the number? You hit.</li>
      <li><strong>Critical hit:</strong> if your roll falls within your crit range, double your damage dice. A roll of <strong>00</strong> is always a fumble &mdash; the weapon slips, the gun jams, something goes wrong. The GM decides what.</li>
      <li><strong>Roll damage</strong> using your weapon's dice.</li>
      <li><strong>Subtract armor soak.</strong> Use the <strong>Ballistic</strong> rating against firearms and high-velocity projectiles, or the <strong>Martial</strong> rating against melee weapons, brawling, and low-velocity attacks. Whatever's left fills their hit point track.</li>
      <li><strong>Check for armor degradation.</strong> If the raw damage <em>exceeded</em> the relevant soak rating, there's a <strong>25% chance</strong> the armor loses 1 point from that rating permanently. Crushing weapons raise this to <strong>50%</strong>. Armor wears down. Every fight costs something.</li>
    </ol>

    {% example %}
      <p>Kael slashes with his hunting knife at a thug wearing a trench coat (Ballistic 1, Martial 2). He rolls 31 against
      Melee 52 &mdash; hit. Damage: 1d4 = 4. It's a melee attack, so the coat's Martial rating applies &mdash;
      absorbs 2, leaving 2 damage into the thug's HP. Since 4 exceeded Martial 2, the GM rolls
      for degradation &mdash; 25% chance. Rolls 18. Bad luck &mdash; the coat splits. Its Martial
      rating drops to 1. Next hit, it'll barely help.</p>
    {% endexample %}

    <h3 id="attack-modifiers">Attack Modifiers</h3>

    <p>
      Not every shot is clean. Not every swing is uncontested. These modifiers stack with
      each other and with wound penalties.
    </p>

    <table>
      <tr><th>Situation</th><th>Modifier</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Target in partial cover</td><td>-10%</td><td>Behind a barrel, around a corner</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Target in heavy cover</td><td>-20%</td><td>Arrow slit, overturned table fortress</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Firing into melee</td><td>-10%</td><td>Near-miss may hit an ally</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Far range</td><td>-10%</td><td>Unless weapon has Accurate tag</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Distant range</td><td>-20%</td><td>Accurate weapons and rifles only</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Aimed shot</td><td>+10%</td><td>Costs +2 speed (you take your time)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wound penalties</td><td>Varies</td><td>Stack with everything</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Exhaustion penalties</td><td>Varies</td><td>Stack with everything</td></tr>
    </table>

    {% gmnote "Firing into Melee" %}
      <p>When you shoot into a Close-range scuffle and miss, there's a real chance you hit
      your friend instead. The GM determines who catches the stray shot. If Sera fires a
      bolt into the tangle where Kael is fighting, she'd better not miss.</p>
    {% endgmnote %}

    <h3 id="active-defense">Active Defense</h3>

    {% spot "knife", "In a sunlit courtyard, a knife fighter lunges at a gunman still drawing his revolver" %}

    <p>
      You don't have to stand there and take it. When an attack comes in, you can try to
      get out of the way &mdash; but it costs you.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Requirements:</strong> you must not be mid-action (your token can't be on the
      track). You get <strong>one</strong> active defense per count.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Choose your skill:</strong> Melee (parry the blow), Brawl (slip and redirect),
      or Athletics (dodge entirely). This is an <strong>opposed check</strong> &mdash; compare
      your margin of success against the attacker's margin. Defender wins ties. If you win,
      no damage. If you lose, the hit lands as normal.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>The cost:</strong> whether you succeed or fail, your next action takes
      <strong>+2 speed</strong>. You spent precious time reacting instead of acting.
      Defending keeps you alive. It also keeps you on the back foot.
    </p>

    <h4>Parrying Weapons</h4>

    <p>
      Weapons with the <strong>Parrying</strong> tag &mdash; rapiers, basket-hilted swords &mdash;
      grant <strong>+10%</strong> when you use Melee for active defense. They're built for it.
    </p>

    <h4>Shields</h4>

    <p>
      Shields add their soak value <strong>passively</strong> against attacks from the front and
      flanks &mdash; Ballistic or Martial, whichever applies. You don't need to do anything
      &mdash; you're holding the shield, it's doing work. You can <em>also</em> actively block
      with an Athletics check for a chance to negate the hit entirely, same rules as active defense.
    </p>

    {% example %}
      <p>Kael's shot resolves at count 7. On count 8, Thug B lunges at him with a knife.
      Kael's not mid-action &mdash; he just resolved &mdash; so he can defend. He uses Melee
      (skill 52) to parry with his own blade. Kael rolls 27 (margin 25). Thug B rolled 22 against Melee 45
      (margin 23). Kael's margin wins &mdash; steel scrapes steel and the thrust goes wide. But his
      next action now costs +2 speed. That parry saved him damage but cost him time.</p>
    {% endexample %}

    <h3 id="range-bands">Range Bands</h3>

    <p>
      No rulers, no grids. Distance is described in four bands. If you can picture the
      scene, you can run the fight.
    </p>

    <table>
      <tr><th>Band</th><th>Description</th><th>What Works Here</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Close</td><td>Arm's reach</td><td>Melee weapons, brawling. This is where swords live.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Near</td><td>Same room, across a street</td><td>Ranged weapons at no penalty. Free movement within this band.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Far</td><td>Across a field, end of a corridor</td><td>-10% ranged (unless Accurate). Sprint to close.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Distant</td><td>Edge of visibility</td><td>-20% ranged. Only Accurate weapons and rifles.</td></tr>
    </table>

    {% street %}
      <p>Close range, blades rule. Near range, pistols. Far range, rifles. Distant, you'd better have a scope or a spell. On the gradient, range shifts with the zone — a gun that reaches Far in neutral might barely manage Near when the Aether thickens. Know your distances. They change.</p>
    {% endstreet %}

    <h4>Movement</h4>

    <p>
      Moving within your current band is free &mdash; you're already there. Moving
      <strong>one band closer or farther</strong> requires a <strong>Sprint</strong>
      action: speed 3. You can't attack and close distance in the same action unless
      you're already at Close range making a melee attack.
    </p>

    <h4>Scatter Weapons</h4>

    <p>
      Shotguns and other weapons with the <strong>Scatter</strong> tag hit harder up close
      and lose punch at range. At Close range, add <strong>+1d6</strong> damage. At Far
      range, subtract <strong>-1d6</strong> damage. They're room-clearers, not sniper tools.
    </p>

    <h4>Taking Cover</h4>

    <p>
      Partial cover gives attackers <strong>-10%</strong>. Heavy cover gives <strong>-20%</strong>.
      You can spend a <strong>speed 2 action</strong> to Take Cover &mdash; diving behind a wall,
      flipping a table, ducking into a doorway. After that, you have cover until you leave it.
    </p>

    <h3 id="weapons">Weapons &amp; Armor</h3>

    <p>
      Full weapon tables, armor ratings, weapon tags, and Galvanic oddities are in
      <a href="equipment.html">Arms &amp; Equipment</a>. Here's what matters for combat:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Every weapon has a <strong>speed</strong> (counts on the timing track) and <strong>damage</strong> (dice rolled on hit).</li>
      <li>Armor has two soak ratings: <strong>Ballistic</strong> (firearms) and <strong>Martial</strong> (melee). Use whichever matches the attack.</li>
      <li>If raw damage exceeds soak, there's a <strong>25% chance</strong> the armor loses 1 soak point. <strong>Crushing</strong> weapons raise this to 50%.</li>
      <li>Weapons with the <strong>Parrying</strong> tag grant +10% on active defense.</li>
      <li>Shields add soak <strong>passively</strong> against frontal attacks.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="malfunction">The Malfunction System</h3>

    {% spot "misfire", "A hand in a leather sleeve holds a revolver down a dark alley as it misfires in a burst of amber sparks" %}

    <p class="flavor">
      Magic and machinery don't mix. The more sorcery saturates the air, the more your
      finely machined firearm becomes an expensive paperweight &mdash; or worse.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every firearm shot is a two-step process. <strong>First, roll Reliability</strong>
      &mdash; does the weapon fire at all? <strong>Then roll Firearms</strong> &mdash; does
      the shot hit? The weapon has to work before your aim matters.
      <strong>Every shot, every time</strong> &mdash; even in clean air.
    </p>

    <p>
      Reliability works like any other d100 roll-under check. Roll at or under your
      weapon's effective Reliability and it fires. Roll over and it malfunctions &mdash;
      shot doesn't fire, action wasted.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Aetheric/Galvanic balance shifts effective Reliability by <strong>2 per net
      point</strong>. Net Aetheric makes guns less reliable. Net Galvanic makes them
      more reliable. At effective Reliability 100+, the weapon <em>cannot</em> fail.
      At 0 or below, it simply doesn't function.
    </p>

    {% example %}
      <p>Thug A fires his revolver (Reliability 95) in an area with net +4 Aetheric.
      Effective Reliability: 95 &minus; 8 = <strong>87</strong>.
      He rolls 91 &mdash; over 87, malfunction. He rolls d10 on the severity table
      and gets a 3 &mdash; <em>Click</em>. The hammer falls on nothing. He can clear it
      and try again next action, but Kael is still coming.</p>
      <p>If he'd rolled 72, that's under 87 &mdash; the weapon fires. Now he rolls Ranged
      to see if the shot hits.</p>
      <p>Later, the party clears out a Galvanic lab (net &minus;8). That same revolver has
      effective Reliability 95 + 16 = <strong>111</strong>. It <em>cannot malfunction</em>.
      The Engine is holding every mechanism in the building together.</p>
    {% endexample %}

    <h4>Malfunction Severity</h4>

    <p>
      When a weapon malfunctions, roll d10 to see how bad it is. Galvanic weapons add
      <strong>+1</strong> to the severity roll.
    </p>

    <table>
      <tr><th>d10</th><th>Result</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
      <tr><td>1&ndash;5</td><td>Click</td><td>Didn't fire. Clear it and try again next action.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6&ndash;7</td><td>Jam</td><td>Weapon is jammed. Clearing takes the weapon's reload time.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Misfire</td><td>A stray shot fires in a random direction. Someone nearby might have a bad day.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9</td><td>Mechanical Failure</td><td>Something broke internally. Weapon needs proper repair &mdash; it's done for this fight.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10+</td><td>Catastrophic</td><td>The weapon is destroyed. Shrapnel deals 1d6 damage to the wielder.</td></tr>
    </table>

    {% warning "Galvanic Weapons Are Fragile" %}
      <p>Galvanic weapons add +1 to the malfunction severity roll. That means a roll of 9 becomes
      10 &mdash; catastrophic. Weapons with the Unstable keyword add +2 instead. That Galvanic Lance
      is magnificent until it detonates on your shoulder. This is the trade-off for superior
      firepower.</p>
    {% endwarning %}

    <h3 id="fight-in-the-alley">A Fight in the Alley</h3>

    <p>
      Let's put it all together. This is a full worked example &mdash; two player characters
      against two armed thugs.
    </p>

    {% scene "The Setup" %}
      <p><strong>Kael</strong> (gunfighter, PC +2, Firearms 66, revolver with the Reliable tag,
      leather jacket B0/M1, 8 HP/tier) and
      <strong>Sera</strong> (wild caster, PC +0, casting target 64, 6 HP/tier) are caught in an
      alley by two thugs with revolvers (Firearms 50, Reliability 95, 7 HP/tier, no armor).
      Range: Near. No prior Aetheric accumulation.</p>
    {% endscene %}

    <p>
      <strong>Count 0 &mdash; First declarations (all simultaneous).</strong>
      Nobody has seen anyone act yet. Everyone commits blind.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Kael:</strong> draws his revolver (speed 2, token at count 2)</li>
      <li><strong>Sera:</strong> begins casting Force (casting time 2, token at count 2)</li>
      <li><strong>Thug A:</strong> fires revolver at Kael (speed 3, token at count 3)</li>
      <li><strong>Thug B:</strong> fires revolver at Sera (speed 3, token at count 3)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Count 2 &mdash; Kael and Sera resolve simultaneously.</strong> Both started at
      count 0, so they go together. Kael's revolver clears leather.
    </p>

    <p>
      Sera's Force spell fires. She rolls 2d100 (wild caster): a <strong>46</strong> and an
      <strong>82</strong>. Her target is 64. The 46 is 18 under; the 82 is 18 over. Equal
      distance &mdash; she takes the 46. Margin 18, that's Standard tier. She could suppress
      it to Weak (her target of 64 allows 2 tiers of suppression), but Standard Force does
      2d6 damage. She keeps it. Targets Thug B. Damage: 2d6 = 8. Sera pays 4 exhaustion.
      Backlash check: 15% chance at Standard for a wild caster. She rolls 67 &mdash; no backlash. The area
      gains <strong>+2 Aetheric accumulation</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Both need new actions, so they declare in PC order: Sera (PC +0) first, then Kael (PC +2).
      Sera begins another Force at Thug A (token at count 4). Kael, seeing that, lines up a shot
      on Thug A too (speed 3, token at count 5). Better to finish one man than wing two.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Count 3 &mdash; Both thugs fire.</strong> Both started at count 0, so they
      resolve simultaneously. Sera's spell already went off at count 2 &mdash; the area is at
      <strong>+2 Aetheric</strong>. Both revolvers are at effective Reliability
      95 &minus; 4 = <strong>91</strong>. Barely degraded, but the clock is ticking.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Thug A shoots Kael:</strong> Reliability check first &mdash; rolls 44 against 91.
        The revolver fires. Firearms roll: 44 against skill 50. Hit. Damage: 2d6 = 7.
        That's a firearm &mdash; Kael's leather jacket has Ballistic 0. No protection. The 7 damage
        fills 7 of the 8 boxes in his OK tier. He's standing, but one more scratch drops him to
        Harmed. <em>He really needs a ballistic vest.</em>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Thug B shoots Sera:</strong> Reliability check &mdash; rolls 38 against 91. Fires.
        Firearms roll: 71 against skill 50. Miss. The bullet sparks off the alley wall.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Count 3 &mdash; New declarations.</strong> Only the thugs need to choose (tied PC,
      same AW &mdash; simultaneous). Kael and Sera are already committed.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Thug A:</strong> fires again at Kael (speed 3, token at 6)</li>
      <li><strong>Thug B:</strong> took 8 damage. 7 fills his OK tier, 1 overflows to Harmed.
        He's at <strong>-10%</strong>. Fires at Sera anyway (skill now 40, token at 6).</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Count 4 &mdash; Sera's second Force.</strong> She rolls 2d100: a <strong>49</strong>
      and a <strong>93</strong>. The 93 is farther from 64 (29 away vs 15 away). Wild casters
      take the farther die &mdash; she's stuck with the <strong>93</strong>. That's over her
      target. <strong>Misfire.</strong> The Force spell rebounds &mdash; Sera pays 2 exhaustion
      and takes 1d6 = 3 damage as invisible force slams her backward into the alley wall.
      Half her OK tier, gone. Misfires still check for backlash: she rolls 72 &mdash; no backlash
      this time, but the magic already bit her. No accumulation &mdash; the spell didn't manifest,
      just went wrong. Area stays at <strong>+2 Aetheric</strong>.
      <em>This is the wild caster's bargain &mdash; access to every spell in your schools,
      but the dice can betray you. And when they do, it hurts.</em>
    </p>

    <p>
      Sera declares: she scrambles for the coal bin behind her (Take Cover, speed 2, token at 6).
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Count 5 &mdash; Kael fires.</strong> Reliability first. The balance is +2, so a plain
      revolver sits at 91 &mdash; but Kael's has the <strong>Reliable</strong> tag, +5 in magic-touched
      air: effective <strong>96</strong>. He rolls <strong>93</strong>. A thug's revolver would have
      clicked on that roll. Kael's fires. Firearms roll: 22 against 66. Hit &mdash; margin 44,
      nowhere near his crit range. Damage: 2d6 = 7. Thug A has no armor; 7 fills his OK tier
      exactly. One more hit and he's hurting.
    </p>

    <p>
      Kael declares again: another shot at Thug A (speed 3, token at count 8).
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Count 6 &mdash; Three tokens.</strong> The thugs started their shots at count 3;
      Sera started her dive at count 4. Whoever started first resolves first &mdash; so the shots
      land before she reaches cover.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Thug A shoots Kael:</strong> Reliability check &mdash; rolls 61 against 91. Fires.
        Firearms roll: 61 against 50. Miss.</li>
      <li><strong>Thug B shoots Sera:</strong> Reliability check &mdash; rolls 55 against 91. Fires.
        Firearms roll: 55 against 40 (wound penalty). Miss.</li>
      <li><strong>Sera</strong> reaches the coal bin. Partial cover: &minus;10% to anyone shooting at her.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Count 6 &mdash; Thug B gets desperate.</strong> He's Harmed, his partner is taking
      fire, and there's a wild caster throwing bolts of force down the alley. He reaches into his
      coat and pulls out a small brass device covered in coiled wire &mdash; a
      <strong>Galvanic force generator</strong>. He flips the switch. The air hums. The
      streetlights flicker.
    </p>

    <p>
      The generator is a sustained Galvanic device. Every <strong>3 counts</strong>, it produces
      <strong>&minus;2 Galvanic shift</strong>, pulling the balance back toward neutral. It
      comes up at count 7 &mdash; its first pulse hits at count 10.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Count 10 &mdash; The generator pulses.</strong> The balance was +2 Aetheric.
      The generator pulls it to <strong>0</strong>. Neutral. The revolvers are back to full
      Reliability <strong>95</strong>. Sera's casting target is back to her base
      <strong>64</strong>. One pulse and the advantage from her first spell is gone.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Count 13 &mdash; Another pulse.</strong> Balance drops to <strong>&minus;2
      Galvanic</strong>. Now the Engine is winning. Every revolver in the alley is at effective
      Reliability 95 + 4 = <strong>99</strong> &mdash; nearly impossible to jam. Sera's casting target
      drops to 64 &minus; 4 = <strong>60</strong>. Her spells are harder to land and her
      wild dice just got crueler. If the generator keeps running, count 16 pushes to &minus;4:
      Reliability <strong>103</strong> (cannot malfunction), casting target <strong>56</strong>.
      The Veil is being smothered.
    </p>

    <p>
      Kael eyes the humming device on Thug B's belt. <em>Here's the twist: it's helping him.
      His revolver has never been more certain. But every pulse chokes Sera a little more. Shoot
      the man, or put a bullet through the brass and give her back the air?</em>
    </p>

    {% gmnote "What Just Happened" %}
      <p>Six counts of combat, then the battlefield flipped. The thugs went from losing
      the environmental war to winning it &mdash; not with better aim, but with a piece of
      Galvanic hardware. The generator didn't fire a shot. It just changed the rules of the
      fight.</p>
      <p>This is the tug-of-war in action. Sera's spells push the balance Aetheric, making guns
      worse and magic better. The generator pushes it Galvanic, making guns better and magic
      worse. Kael's hunting knife doesn't care either way &mdash; but his revolver does, and this
      time the Engine is on his side while it's strangling his partner. The smart play isn't
      always "hit harder." Sometimes it's "destroy the thing that's changing the air," even when
      the thing is helping you.</p>
    {% endgmnote %}
